<title>USA: U.S. Nov wholesale inventories rose 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Nov wholesale inventories rose 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce Department report of</p>
<p>wholesale inventories and sales, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p> (Percent Changes)     Nov    Oct--Rev from  Nov96/95</p>
<p> Inventories Total     0.1    0.3     0.6     1.3</p>
<p> Sales Total	     0.9    0.7     0.3     6.9</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)    Nov    Oct--Rev from   Nov95</p>
<p> Inventories Total  258.02  257.77  258.61   254.69</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     163.58  163.17  163.73   161.64</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  27.59   27.48   27.86    27.46</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  94.43   94.60   94.88    93.04</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.   5.49    5.44    5.52     4.82</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)    Nov    Oct--Rev from   Nov95</p>
<p> Sales Total	  204.58  202.79  201.98   191.35</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     105.35  103.82  103.22    99.51</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  16.86   16.91   16.86    16.72</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  99.23   98.97   98.77    91.84</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.  16.95   16.21   16.01    12.91</p>
<p>The November stock-to-sales ratio for merchant wholesalers, seasonally adjusted, fell to 1.26 months' worth, the lowest level since March 1990, from a revised 1.27 months' in October. October was previously reported at 1.28 months.</p>
<p>The ratio is a measure of how many months it would take to sell existing inventories at the current sales pace.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast wholesale inventories to rise 0.2 percent in November.</p>
<p>The 0.9 percent rise in November sales was the biggest increase since a 1.8 percent rise in July.</p>
</text>
